{"schema_version":1,"course":"tourist-sv","unit":"food-and-drink","language":"en","target_language":"sv","phrases":[{"concept":"one-coffee-please","text":{"Latn":"One coffee, please"}},{"concept":"water-please","text":{"Latn":"A glass of water, please"}},{"concept":"table-for-two","text":{"Latn":"A table for two, please"}},{"concept":"the-menu-please","text":{"Latn":"The menu, please"}},{"concept":"the-bill-please","text":{"Latn":"The bill, please"}},{"concept":"i-dont-eat-meat","text":{"Latn":"I don't eat meat"}},{"concept":"without-nuts-please","text":{"Latn":"Without nuts, please"}},{"concept":"what-do-you-recommend","text":{"Latn":"What do you recommend?"}},{"concept":"delicious","text":{"Latn":"Delicious!"}},{"concept":"a-beer-please","text":{"Latn":"A beer, please"}},{"concept":"cheers","text":{"Latn":"Cheers!"}}],"glosses":[{"lemma":"sv:en","gloss":"a, an; one","note":"Swedish has two genders: “en” for most words (en kaffe) and “ett” for the rest (ett glas). Learn each noun with its article."},{"lemma":"sv:kaffe","gloss":"coffee"},{"lemma":"sv:tack","gloss":"thank you; please","note":"Means “thank you”, and after a request also “please”: “En kaffe, tack.”"},{"lemma":"sv:glas","gloss":"glass","note":"“Ett glas vatten” is “a glass of water”: Swedish needs no word for “of”."},{"lemma":"sv:vatten","gloss":"water"},{"lemma":"sv:bord","gloss":"table"},{"lemma":"sv:for","gloss":"for"},{"lemma":"sv:tva","gloss":"two"},{"lemma":"sv:meny","gloss":"menu"},{"lemma":"sv:nota","gloss":"bill"},{"lemma":"sv:jag","gloss":"I","note":"“Mig” is the object form: “Hjälp mig” (help me)."},{"lemma":"sv:ata","gloss":"to eat"},{"lemma":"sv:inte","gloss":"not","note":"It comes after the verb: “Jag förstår inte.”"},{"lemma":"sv:kott","gloss":"meat"},{"lemma":"sv:utan","gloss":"without"},{"lemma":"sv:not","gloss":"nut"},{"lemma":"sv:vad","gloss":"what"},{"lemma":"sv:rekommendera","gloss":"to recommend"},{"lemma":"sv:du","gloss":"you (informal, singular)","note":"Swedes say “du” to almost everyone, strangers and bosses included. “Ni” is for several people or, rarely, very formal."},{"lemma":"sv:god","gloss":"good","note":"“God” goes with en-words (God dag, God kväll); ett-words take “gott”. “Gott!” on its own means “Tasty!”."},{"lemma":"sv:ol","gloss":"beer"},{"lemma":"sv:skal","gloss":"cheers!","note":"Literally “bowl”; the toast is “Skål!”."}]}
